## Getting started

```sh
just install          # uv sync
just up               # Postgres, Redis and Mailpit, if you are not already running them
cp .env.example .env  # optional; every value has a working default
just migrate          # apply migrations from the host; `just dev` also does it on start
just dev              # {% if service_shape == 'api' %}the API in a container: http://localhost:8000/docs{% elif service_shape == 'worker' %}the worker in a container{% else %}the API on :8000 and the worker, in containers{% endif %}
```

`just dev` runs the application the way it deploys: in containers next to
Postgres, Redis and Mailpit, from this directory mounted in, so it is the same
command on macOS, Linux and Windows. Every message the application sends lands
in Mailpit, at http://localhost:{{ mailpit_ui_port }}; nothing leaves the machine. {% if has_api %}The API reloads when a file changes{% if has_worker %}; the worker
does not, so after changing a job run `docker compose restart worker`{% endif %}.
`just serve` runs the API on the host instead, for a debugger.{% else %}The worker does not reload; after
changing a job run `docker compose restart worker`, or run it on the host with
`just worker`.{% endif %}

`just` on its own runs the gate: `lint`, `types`, `test`.

Postgres is published on `{{ postgres_port }}` and Redis on `{{ redis_port }}`, because 5432 and 6379
are usually already taken. If the Keel workspace's own stack is running on those
ports, skip `just up`: the credentials match.

## Layout

Package by domain, not by layer. Everything about a feature is in one directory,
and the layers inside it point one way only.

```
app/
{% if has_api %}  main.py          app factory; the lifespan wires every subsystem
{% endif %}{% if has_worker %}  worker.py        the worker entrypoint{% if has_api %}; the same lifespans, minus tokens{% else %}; the same lifespans as a script{% endif %}
  healthcheck.py   `python -m app.healthcheck` — the worker's exec probe
{% endif %}  settings.py      pydantic-settings -> Keel's plain dataclass configs
  errors.py        the error taxonomy{% if has_api %}, and RFC 9457 problem+json for it{% endif %}
{% if has_api %}  security.py      the bearer-token dependency: Caller and MaybeCaller
  observability.py the request id: bound for the request, returned on the reply
  inspector.py     /_inspector under DEBUG: each request as a timeline of what it did
{% endif %}  policies.py      who may do what, per resource type
  modules/
    __init__.py    the registry: {% if has_api %}MODULES{% if has_worker %} and JOBS{% endif %}{% else %}JOBS{% endif %}
{% if has_api %}    sessions/      sign in, sign out, who am I — no table of its own
{% endif %}    users/         models · schemas · repository · service{% if has_api %} · router{% endif %} · mail{% if has_worker %} · jobs{% endif %}
    items/         the same files{% if has_worker %}, plus jobs.py{% endif %}, scoped by owner_id
    password_resets/  a single-use code, hashed at rest; the change path for somebody who cannot sign in
  mail.py          the one MailTemplates over templates/mail; every module's mail.py renders through it
  templates/mail/  a .txt.j2 and a .html.j2 per message; layout.html.j2 is the look of all of them
  migrations/      async Alembic, target_metadata = Model.metadata
tests/             rollback-per-test against a real Postgres
```

{% if has_api and has_worker %}    router  ->
                 service  ->  repository  ->  models
    handler ->   business      queries       tables
                  logic
{% elif has_api %}    router  ->  service  ->  repository  ->  models
      HTTP      business      queries       tables
                  logic
{% else %}    handler ->  service  ->  repository  ->  models
     a job     business      queries       tables
                 logic
{% endif %}
* **`models` and `schemas` never import each other.** The table shape and the
  wire shape are allowed to differ, and a column rename should not be a breaking
  API change nobody reviewed.
* **A repository takes a session; it never opens one.**
* **A service opens `uow()` and returns schemas.** Never an ORM object — its
  session has closed by the time the router has it.
* **A message is a template pair, named from `mail.py` and sent from a seam
  that waits for the commit.** `app/templates/mail/<name>.txt.j2` is
  mandatory and `<name>.html.j2` extends `layout.html.j2`; a module's
  `mail.py` names the pair, the recipients and the subject. Registration {% if has_worker %}dispatches `SendWelcome`, which the queue pushes only once
  the transaction commits{% else %}registers the send with `after_commit`, so it runs only once
  the transaction commits{% endif %}. A welcome to a row that rolled back would be a
  welcome to nobody.
{% if has_api %}* **A router calls services.** No session, no repository, no SQLAlchemy import.
{% endif %}{% if has_worker %}* **A job handler calls the same services a router does.** It is a second entry
  into the domain, not a second copy of it.
{% endif %}
## The rule that shapes all of it

There is no `get_session` dependency, in Keel or here, and adding one would be a
mistake rather than a convenience.

FastAPI PR #12066 — open since August 2024, with production outages in its
comments — means a session yielded from `Depends` stays open *through*
response-model validation. Under load the pool starves while every individual
request still looks fast at p99. So the transaction spans the work, not the
request:

```python
async def create_user(payload: UserCreate) -> UserRead:
    async with uow() as session:  # opens here
        users = UserRepository(session)
        user = await users.create(...)
        return UserRead.model_validate(user)  # closes here, before the response
```

{% if has_api %}## Signing in

Three endpoints, on a resource rather than on three verbs:

| | | |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /sessions` | sign in | returns a bearer token, once |
| `GET /sessions/current` | who am I | the caller's own user record |
| `DELETE /sessions/current` | sign out | revokes that one token |

And two more for the person who cannot sign in, both open and both silent
about whether the address exists:

| | | |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /password-resets` | request a code | 202 and no body either way; a message goes out only to a registered, active address, once a minute |
| `POST /password-resets/redeem` | set a new password | 204 with the code from the message, once; every session is signed out |

The code is 32 bytes of `secrets` output and the table keeps its SHA-256, like
the token store. It is not a bearer token and is not kept in the token store,
because a store entry resolves to an identity and a reset code in it would sign
the holder in. Redeeming is one `UPDATE ... WHERE used_at IS NULL` statement,
which is what makes a code single-use under concurrency, and every path that
touches an account and its codes locks the account row first, which is what
keeps two of them from deadlocking. A password change retires every
outstanding code. The code travels in the request body, not the path, because
the access log and the inspector record paths. The reply says nothing about
whether the address exists; the request's timing does, and so does the 409
one route over, which is why that is recorded rather than closed.

Sign-in is throttled: five wrong passwords for one address from one client
in a minute, and the sixth answers 429 with a `Retry-After`, before any hash
runs. A correct password clears the window. `POST /password-resets` allows
ten requests a minute per client, whatever the addresses. The client is the
peer address as ASGI saw it; behind a reverse proxy, run uvicorn with
`--proxy-headers` and `--forwarded-allow-ips` naming the proxy, or every
client shares one window. `app/security.py` says why the header is never
read directly.

A token is 256 bits of `secrets` output and the store keeps only its SHA-256, so
nothing — including this application — can hand a working credential back. That
is the opposite rule from passwords, which are hashed with Argon2 precisely
*because* they are guessable, and it is deliberate: a slow hash on every
authenticated request would buy nothing against a value with no dictionary
behind it.

Guarding an endpoint is one annotation:

```python
from app.security import Caller, MaybeCaller


@router.get("/drafts")
async def list_drafts(caller: Caller) -> list[DraftRead]:  # 401 without a token
    ...


@router.get("/posts")
async def list_posts(caller: MaybeCaller) -> list[PostRead]:  # None is fine
    ...
```

Both bind the caller on a context variable for the whole request, so a service
or a job reads it with `require_identity()` instead of receiving it as an
argument. What is bound is a Keel `Identity` — a frozen value with an id, roles
and claims — and never the `User` row, which would be detached from its session
the moment the transaction closed.

**The sign-in path has one rule that is easy to get wrong**, and
`app/modules/sessions/service.py` is commented for it: when there is no such
address, the stored hash handed to the verifier is `None` rather than the
function returning early. Verifying nothing is instant and verifying Argon2 is
not, so an early return lets anyone with a stopwatch ask which addresses have
accounts. The reply is uniform for the same reason — a wrong password and an
unknown address get the same 401 with the same message.

The verifier also hands back a replacement hash when the stored one was made
with older cost parameters, and the service persists it. Signing in is the only
moment the plaintext exists to do that with.

**The hash itself runs on a worker thread, outside every unit of work.** Argon2
is ~40 ms of CPU by design; called inline from `async def` it stalls every other
request in the process for that long, and called inside `uow()` it does so while
holding a pooled connection that needs nothing from the database. So `sign_in`
reads the row, closes the block, `await anyio.to_thread.run_sync(...)`, then
reopens a block only when there is an upgraded hash to persist. Two transactions
per request is normal; a connection parked across 40 ms of arithmetic is not.

## Who may call what

Two questions, answered in two places. **Is somebody there** is the route's:
`POST /users` is registration and is open, and every other route takes `Caller`
from `app/security.py`, which answers 401 when no valid token was presented.
**May that somebody** is a policy's, and lives in `app/policies.py` — see the
next section.

| | | |
|---|---|---|
| `POST /users` | open | registration has to work without a credential |
| `GET /users` | `Caller` + `list` on the directory | an admin; a listing of every address is the enumeration answer |
| `GET /users/{pid}` | `Caller` + `view` | the owner, or an admin |
| `PATCH`, `DELETE /users/{pid}` | `Caller` + `update` / `delete` | the owner, or an admin |
| `PUT /users/{pid}/password` | `Caller` + `change-password` + the current password | the owner alone |
| `/items` | `Caller` | the caller's own; the session names the owner, so the URL does not |
| `/users/{owner_pid}/items` | `Caller` + the items policy | the owner, or an admin acting for them |

The password lives on its own route rather than in `UserUpdate` because a
password set without proving knowledge of the old one is a takeover primitive
even behind authentication — and it is the one ability an admin does not get,
because the precondition is the password in force and a role is not knowledge
of it.

Changing a password revokes every session the user has, and so does disabling or
deleting the account: `token_store().revoke_subject(user_id)`. A credential
change that leaves already-issued tokens working has changed nothing that an
attacker holding one would notice.

{% endif %}## Authorization

`app/policies.py` is the whole of it: two frozen reference values, two rules,
and two lines registering them.

```python
def items_policy(identity: Identity | None, action: str, items: ItemsOf) -> bool:
    if identity is None:
        return False
    match action:
        case "view" | "create" | "update" | "delete":
            return identity.id == items.owner_id or identity.has_role(ADMIN)
        case _:
            return False
```

**A rule per resource type, chosen by Keel's registry.** `authorize("update",
ItemsOf(owner_id))` looks the rule up by the class of the thing being acted on
and asks it. Adding a resource is a function and a line; no existing rule grows
a branch.

**Nothing is permitted by omission.** A resource type nobody registered is
*refused*, and loudly — `authorize` raises rather than answering 403, because a
missing registration is a wiring bug and a 403 would file it where nobody looks.
Deleting a policy breaks its callers; it does not open them.

**The check is in the service, not at the edge.** Every function in
`app/modules/*/service.py` that acts on a named resource opens with `authorize`,
before its unit of work. A guard on the HTTP layer protects only the callers who
arrive over HTTP{% if has_worker %}, and a job handler is not one of them{% endif %}; a refusal before the block
also means no transaction is opened and no connection borrowed. `create_user` is
the one function that does not authorize, because registration cannot.

**A resource is a reference, not a row.** `Account(user_id)` and
`ItemsOf(owner_id)` carry a primary key and nothing else. A session is never
held across a request, so a route has no ORM object to judge. Register a policy
for a model class and pass the loaded object when a rule genuinely needs the row.

**Two identifiers, one of them public.** Every row a route can name carries a
`pid` beside its primary key (`keel.database.PublicId`): a random UUID, the
only identifier in a URL or a response. The primary key is time-ordered for the
index and never leaves the process, and the caller's own routes — `/items`,
`GET /sessions/current` — need no identifier at all, because the session names
them. A service resolves the `pid` a route named to the row, then authorizes
against the row's primary key; a stranger holding a `pid` was given it, and a
403 tells them nothing they did not have.

**Roles come from you.** `Identity.roles` is whatever the edge put there when
the token was minted, and this scaffold mints none: give your users a role
column or a membership table and fill it in at that moment. The `admin` arms in
the two policies are written and waiting.
{% if has_worker %}
**A job says who it acts for.** `IndexItem.handle` wraps its work in
`acting_as(Identity(id=owner_id))`, rebuilt from the payload, and the same rule
applies to it as to a request. It carries no roles on purpose: a job must never
be able to do more than the person it acts for, or the queue becomes an
escalation path.
{% endif %}
{% if has_worker %}## The queue

`app/modules/items/jobs.py` is the example worth reading. Three things in it are
the whole convention:

```python
@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class IndexItem(Job):
    # Policy on the class, never on the wire; ids in the payload, never rows.
    max_attempts: ClassVar[int] = 5
    item_id: str

    async def handle(self) -> None:
        try:
            # The same function the router calls.
            item = await service.get_item(...)
        except NotFoundError as exc:
            # A 404 to a router; "stop retrying" to a worker.
            raise PermanentFailureError(...) from exc
```

**Dispatch happens inside the unit of work**, in `service.create_item`, so the
push waits for the commit. Enqueue outside the transaction and a worker runs
against a row that may never exist — and usually wins the race, so the job
dead-letters looking like corrupted data rather than like an ordering bug.

**The worker's liveness is a file, not an endpoint.** It refreshes the mtime of
`WORKER_HEALTH_FILE` while it is healthy and deletes it on exit;
`python -m app.healthcheck` exits 0 or 1 on the file's age and is wired as the
compose healthcheck. A draining worker still reports alive — liveness that fails
during a graceful shutdown gets the container killed mid-job. There is no worker
readiness probe: readiness decides where traffic goes, and a worker takes none.

**Its metrics are the one listener it opens, and only when asked.**
`WORKER_METRICS_PORT` serves jobs by name and outcome, faults, the jobs in flight
and how long since the queue last answered — the number that climbs through a
Redis outage while liveness, correctly, stays green. Zero opens nothing.

Exhausted jobs land in `keel_failed_jobs` with the whole envelope and the
original traceback, so they can be retried by hand. `app/worker.py` prunes that
table nightly through `SCHEDULE`, which is also where recurring work goes.

## Configuration

`pydantic-settings`. Each subsystem carries its own prefix rather than a nested
delimiter, so the variables read as `DATABASE_URL`, `CACHE_STORE`,
`HASHING_TIME_COST`, `LOG_LEVEL`, `MAIL_HOST`{% if has_api %}, `TOKEN_DRIVER`{% endif %}, `QUEUE_DRIVER`
and `WORKER_HEALTH_FILE` — the names a hosting platform injects. Keel's own
configs are plain frozen dataclasses — the library takes no configuration-stack
dependency — so `app/settings.py` is the one place that translates between the
two. See `.env.example`.

`CACHE_PREFIX`{% if has_api %}, `TOKEN_PREFIX`{% endif %} and `QUEUE_PREFIX` are **siblings**
(`{{ project_slug }}:cache`{% if has_api %}, `{{ project_slug }}:tokens`{% endif %}, `{{ project_slug }}:queue`) and must stay
that way. They share one Redis, and `cache.flush()` scans its whole prefix — so
a cache namespaced at `{{ project_slug }}` would delete the others' keys. Keel shipped
exactly that bug for two phases.
