## Logging, and the request id

`LOG_FORMAT=json` writes one object per line — `time`, `level`, `logger`,
`message`, then whatever ambient fields were in effect. `text` is the same
content laid out for a terminal. `configure_logging` is called {% if has_api and has_worker %}by both
entrypoints{% elif has_api %}in `create_app`{% else %}in `app.worker.main`{% endif %}, before anything else, because the
failures worth reading happen during start-up.
{% if has_api %}
**Every request gets an id.** `app/observability.py` mints one — or accepts an
inbound `X-Request-ID`, once it has checked the value is short and has no quotes
or newlines in it — binds it for the request, and returns it on the response.
Every line written while that request is being served carries it, including
lines from modules that only ever called `logging.getLogger`.

**Under `DEBUG=true`, every request is also recorded as a timeline.**
`app/inspector.py` serves the last `INSPECTOR_RETAIN` requests at
`/_inspector/requests`: each one's queries with their durations, cache hits and
misses,{% if has_worker %} jobs dispatched and whether they waited for the commit,{% endif %} and the log
lines it wrote. It is the tool for "this endpoint is slow" — open the trace and
count the queries. The endpoint exists only under `DEBUG`, because it shows SQL
and log text to whoever can reach it, and bind parameters stay out unless
`INSPECTOR_PARAMETERS=true`.

**In production, the same sources feed `/metrics`.** Requests by route
template and status with their latency, statements by operation, cache round
trips by outcome{% if has_worker %}, dispatches, jobs by name and outcome{% endif %}, readiness checks by
result, and the process's memory and CPU, in Prometheus' format. No path, id or
statement ever becomes a label. `METRICS_ENABLED=false` leaves it unmounted.
{% endif %}{% if has_worker %}
**And it travels.** `dispatch()` seals the fields in effect onto the envelope,
and the worker binds them again around the attempt. So the `IndexItem` line the
worker writes minutes later{% if has_api %} carries the `request_id` of the `POST` that enqueued
it{% else %} carries whatever the caller bound{% endif %} — one query, both halves:

```sh
{% if has_api %}just dev
curl -s -X POST localhost:8000/items -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" -H "X-Request-ID: demo-1" ...{% else %}just worker{% endif %}
# then, in the logs
grep '"request_id": "demo-1"' ...
```

A dead letter is joinable the same way: `keel_failed_jobs.context` is the same
mapping, in its own column so it can be queried without opening the envelope.

**A retried dead letter keeps its original id** rather than picking up the one
belonging to the operator who pressed retry. The replay is verbatim by design,
and `context["retried_from"]` joins the new dispatch to the old one.
{% endif %}
**Bind a field the same way anywhere**:

```python
from keel.observability import correlate

with correlate(tenant=tenant_id):
    ...
```

It merges with whatever is already bound and restores on the way out. Values are
coerced to strings and `None` is dropped, so `correlate(tenant=maybe)` adds
nothing rather than recording the string `None`. Names that look like a
credential — `password`, `api_key`, `token`, `cookie`, `bearer` — are
**refused**, because a correlation field is written to every log line{% if has_worker %} and sealed
onto every envelope{% endif %}. That is a check on the name, not the value: nothing can
tell that `reference` holds a credential, so do not put one there.

`logging.info("shipped", extra={"shipment": "S1"})` works as well, and lands on
the same line beside the ambient fields. Use `correlate` for anything that
should travel and `extra=` for a fact about one line.

**Every line in the stream has the same shape, uvicorn's included.** The server
runs with `--no-access-log` because `app/observability.py` already writes one
line per request, and `configure_logging` takes uvicorn's own handlers off so
its tracebacks propagate to the root formatter rather than arriving as plain
text.

Nothing puts `Identity.roles` or `Identity.claims` on a record. The principal's
**id** is added as `user_id`, and that is all — `claims` is yours to fill and
would turn the log aggregator into a copy of it.

## Migrations

```sh
just revision "add widgets"   # autogenerate
just migrate                  # alembic upgrade head
```

`target_metadata` is `keel.database.Model.metadata`, which carries Keel's
constraint naming convention. That is what makes autogenerated names
deterministic across backends — and what lets a downgrade name the constraint it
is dropping. New models must be imported in `app/migrations/env.py` or
autogenerate will produce an empty migration and report success.
{% if has_worker %}
Keel's own `FailedJob` and `ScheduleRun` are imported there too, and that import
is load-bearing rather than tidy: without it, autogenerate finds two tables no
model claims and proposes `DROP TABLE` on both — deleting the dead-letter record
and the scheduler's exactly-once ledger, in a diff that reads like cleanup.
{% endif %}
## Tests

```sh
just test
```

Real Postgres, no fake, no SQLite substitute: the queries are the part most
likely to be wrong. Each test runs inside a transaction that is rolled back, via
`keel.testing.rolled_back_database`, so the suite needs no truncation and no
re-seeding. Application code commits normally — sessions join the outer
transaction with `join_transaction_mode="create_savepoint"`, so a commit releases
a savepoint.

Two things follow from that and will eventually surprise someone:

* Every row written in one test shares a `created_at`, because Postgres' `now()`
  is the transaction timestamp. Assert on sets, not on timestamp order.
* The cache is switched to the in-process `array` driver, so the suite needs
  Postgres but not Redis. Use `keel.testing.fake_cache` to assert on caching.
* Mail is switched to `log`, so the suite needs no Mailpit either. A test that
  asks what was sent enters `keel.testing.fake_mail`, which records every
  message and asserts on recipient and subject. See `tests/test_users.py`.
{% if has_api %}* The token store is switched to `memory` for the same reason, and
  `HASHING_*` is turned down to the cheapest parameters Keel accepts. Argon2 at
  production cost is tens of milliseconds and every test that needs a user pays
  it — which is what those knobs are for.
{% endif %}{% if has_worker %}* The queue is switched to `null`, which is a different choice for a different
  reason: `keel.testing.fake_queue` records dispatches without running them, so
  "did this enqueue the job?" and "does the job work?" stay two tests. See
  `tests/test_jobs.py`.
{% endif %}
## Running it in Docker

`just up` starts only Postgres, Redis and Mailpit. `just dev` is `docker compose
--profile app up`, which adds {% if has_api and has_worker %}the API and the worker — the same image with a
different command, which is the whole point of the table above.{% elif has_api %}the API.{% else %}the worker.{% endif %} Each app
container keeps its virtualenv in a named volume, so the `uv sync` on start is
a no-op after the first time. `just down` stops all of it.
{% if has_worker %}
The worker's healthcheck is an *exec* probe rather than an HTTP one, since it
serves no HTTP: `app.healthcheck` exits 0 while the heartbeat file is younger
than `WORKER_HEALTH_MAX_AGE`. That file stays inside the container on purpose —
on a shared volume a dead replica's heartbeat would keep a live replica green.
{% endif %}{% if has_api %}
The API answers two probes. `/health` is liveness and touches nothing, because
restarting a process does not fix its database. `/ready` asks every dependency
in `READINESS` concurrently, under a deadline, and answers 503 naming the one that
failed — add to that mapping whenever you add a lifespan.
{% endif %}
### Why there is no Dockerfile

{% if keel_source == 'path' -%}
Keel is a *path* dependency (`{{ keel_path }}`), which a build context cannot
see. Compose mounts the source instead and lets uv install into a
container-local virtualenv; `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT` keeps that out of `./.venv`,
which holds host binaries that would not run in the container.

Generating from Keel's repository rather than a checkout (`keel new` without
`--source`) removes that constraint: the dependency becomes a pinned git source
a build context can fetch.
{%- else -%}
Compose runs the app from a mounted source tree and lets uv install into a
container-local virtualenv; `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT` keeps that out of `./.venv`,
which holds host binaries that would not run in the container. That is a
development arrangement. Keel is a git dependency pinned to a commit, so a
build context *can* fetch it, and a Dockerfile is a `uv sync --frozen` in an
image away — left to the deployment, because the base image, the user and the
entrypoint are its decisions.
{%- endif %}

## What is deliberately not here yet

* **A source for roles.** The policies read `Identity.roles` and the scaffold
  never fills it: there is no role column, no membership table and no route that
  grants one. That is application design, not framework, and the shape of it
  differs enough between products that a guess here would be one to undo.
* **Rules that apply to every resource.** A super-admin bypass, a tenant check,
  a suspended-account gate — anything that has to run before or after *every*
  policy. That is the point at which a chain of responsibility earns its place;
  a chain with one link would be indirection with a pattern's name on it, so the
  registry answers one rule per resource type until then.
{% if has_api %}* **Refresh tokens.** `revoke` plus `issue` composes rotation today. Pairing
  them is a decision about session policy, not about storage.
* **Roles on the user row.** Application policy rather than framework: the
  policies read `Identity.roles`, and what fills it is yours to decide.
* **Rate limits anywhere but sign-in and reset requests.** Registration and
  the item routes have none. `keel.ratelimit.guard` is one line wherever one
  is wanted; which routes deserve one is a decision about your traffic.
{% endif %}* **A generic base repository.** Two modules is not enough evidence to know which
  methods genuinely repeat. Phase 2.
* **Soft deletes and audit columns.** Listed in the ADR rather than stubbed here;
  a half-built mixin is worse than an absent one.
